A||N||11||MickeyAsh||Recalls||||9||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||10-03-2000||01:26 AM||||I know this issue has probably been pounded repeatedly, but I couldn't find my answer anywhere in the past posts, so I figured I might as well bring it up again. I know there's a major recall for a list of Fieros, including the '86 GT, which involves the exhaust manifolds, and installing a heat shield to prevent coolant and other fluids from making contact with the manifold, causing a fire. However, upon calling 800-PMCARES, they said they "couldn't go back that far", and the only option I had was to take it to the dealer. I took the car to the dealer and their response was that my car didn't fall under the recalled vehicles. I quickly showed them a printed document which listed VINs recalled, and mine fell almost dead in the middle of one set. Her next response was "Well, the computer doesn't show the VIN you gave me to require a recall. That means that since your car is obviously involved in a recall, that either a previous owner had it done, or the recall expired." My response was of course "Safety recalls DON'T expire, and if the previous owner had it done, why does my car still have the splash guard on the decklid, lack a heat shield, still have the tubing that is supposedly removed in the recall, and why don't I have a sticker on my decklid stating that the recall was done?" Her response was "I don't know, but there's nothing we can do." So here's my question to you guys... how many of you have been successful in getting your recalls done, and if you succeeded, how many dealers did you have to go to before finally one decided they could do it? Another question is, if the people behind the 800 number dont have anything about the recall, and the dealers dont have anything about the recall, how the HECK can I get my recalls taken care of?! I'm seriously distressed, lol... I look forward to any responses... thanks in advance.<P>-Black Sheep||||reg||9
Z||000001||ROB MAC||10-03-2000||01:33 PM||||I had the E-brake recall done and later suspected it wasn't completly done. After a few phone calls it went nowhere. If you could get the parts which I am told come with instructions , I think you would do better to do it yourself. I had no trouble with any of the other recalls. When they removed the drip pan from the decklid I put metal window screen in to stop debris from falling into the engine compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MickeyAsh||10-03-2000||02:32 PM||||I just find the denial of service somewhat rediculous. They really aren't losing that much money by doing these recalls, in fact, I'm quite sure pontiac pays the dealers to perform recalls, so they likely are making money off it. Isn't there some kind of lawsuit appropriate for this? lol...<P>-Black Sheep||||reg||8
Z||000003||Fformula88||10-03-2000||05:46 PM||||The dealers get paid, but not as much as if they were charging you.  So if the service dept at the dealer has all the "regular" business it needs, it won't want to do the recall as bad.  I got the recall done in 97 after I bought my Formula.  They called it "the campaign"!  Unfortunately there aren't any stickers running around anymore to anounce that the recall is done (or thats what they told me anyway).  However, I do have the shield and re-routed air pipes.  They were more than happy to do it too.  Try calling a couple other dealers and give them your VIN number.  Someone should be able to help you out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sillhouette.com||10-03-2000||06:02 PM||||I have not had any experience with recalls, but just last week got my hand on an 1986GT that was supposed to have the exhaust manifold problem fixed.  Upon closer inspection I saw that the exhaust bolts were missing and there was an exhaust leak. When tried to replace it I found that the bolt was broken into the cylinder head.  <P>Doing recalls is great and cheap, make sure that it's done properly.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||10-03-2000||10:37 PM||||How can I find out about recalls? All of my Fieros were bought used so I am on no mailing list...<P>Obviously, the two I would worry about most are the 88 coupe and 88 Formula.||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||10-03-2000||11:07 PM||||Like previously stated, call other dealerships and try there. You need to get "firm" with these people that say "well theres nothing we can do"..bullsh1t, there is, thats their job! You MAKE them do it.<BR>Songman, the 88s to my knowledge really were the best (as far as having most of the "bugs" worked out), I wouldnt fret (no pun intended to your username lol) too much about the 88s.||||reg||1
Z||000007||MickeyAsh||10-04-2000||04:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>Like previously stated, call other dealerships and try there. You need to get "firm" with these people that say "well theres nothing we can do"..bullsh1t, there is, thats their job! You MAKE them do it.<BR>Songman, the 88s to my knowledge really were the best (as far as having most of the "bugs" worked out), I wouldnt fret (no pun intended to your username lol) too much about the 88s.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Theres really no way to get firm with them when they say they have no instructions on the recall, or any knowledge of a recall necessary... granted we all know they exist and that they could find the instructions and parts needed, theres not much of a way to prove they're lying. The only thoughts I've come up with are, as Fformula stated, try a few other dealers...||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-04-2000||09:00 AM||||The full text of the recalls is on ALLData. It may be on the Popular Machanics CD's at Wall/K mart. (A sumary of the 4 cyl engine fire one is in my cave.Including the GM document number etc.) They are available to all GM deallers thru GM's service distribution network. Claims that there are no instructions are bogus. They are distributed to all deallers when released but don't be suprised if many are trashed or "improperly" filed.<P>Griping to each other makes you feel better but doesn't solve your problem. Contact your state attourny generals office and NHTSA. (Summaries of recalls can also be found on NHTSA's web site. <A HREF="http://www.nhtsa.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.nhtsa.gov</A>  that will give you release dates and GM document numbers.) NHTSA is under a big lens right now over the tire thing. They may be more help than normal. If they start getting a string of complaints that GM dealers aren't doing recall work they won't have much choice but to look into it. It might not get the work done any sooner but it will make the dealers lives dificult.<P>The dealers are paid for the recall work and the parts. In addition to the parts and instructions for the recall, original dealer instructions include all the reimbursement amounts and instructions to collect it. (I left that out of my summary) ||||reg||1
Z||000009||MickeyAsh||10-05-2000||02:46 AM||||I thank you much, this should give me a little more official information to play around with. I honestly doubt many dealers will try to push an issue if NHTSA is on the phone telling them to do the recall... but still the question arises, what can I say when they say that the computer says the VIN I gave them doesn't require a recall? Even if the NHTSA says "Every Fiero requires this recall", the dealer is going to respond that one of the previous owners had it done without record of it... which is bullsh*t...||||reg||5
Z||000010||Raydar||10-05-2000||07:13 AM||||I wonder how Pontiac would react to a lawsuit that was filed because a car caught fire after a dealer <I>refused</I> to do the recall, on a car that obviously hadn't had it. Try asking the dealer <I>that</I>.<BR>Or not. Just food for thought.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000011||My7Fieros||10-05-2000||07:23 PM||||MickeyAsh, quite being so intimidated by the person behind the desk that says "theres nothing we can do"...I already told you thats b.s. They say that for one of two reasons..1. They're ignorant of the recall situation, basically have no clue or 2. They're just plain lazy.<BR>Tell them there ARE recalls ...SAFTEY recalls that NEVER expire! Tell them you want to talk to their supervisor..raise hell if you have to! Sometimes you've really got to be firm with some people. You're not asking them to wash your car, you're asking (telling) them that you need saftey recalls done on your car..recalls ordered to be done by GM. ||||reg||1
